As you can see the code is very simple as the input is sanitized. This is an exercise for an exam and the code should be very simple.

In the other hand the user has only to provide the code and not the explanation and this is not that bad.

## Why is it useful

So what does this help?

The idea is that if I have a test to develop, instead of writing the code and explaining everything I can write the code and the program will do the rest.

In this case the user will be able to send only the code and I can send the code and the explanation or a part of the code and I can get the complete code if you don't understand everything.

The only restriction is that you need to send one piece of code at time.

Note: there is a bit of a problem with this idea, is that you can't know if the user wrote the solution or not, so if you are the teacher it will be a problem.

## How to setup?

Note: I'm using Eclipse as IDE but it should work in any.

1. Create a new project and add to the library the file [jsoup-1.8.3.jar](http://jsoup.org/download)
2. Add the package in the Eclipse to the library and right click in the package -> Build Path -> Configure Build Path -> Libraries -> Add JARs -> Select the JAR and apply

Now you should be able to see the library in the Eclipse.

