There are several ways to define a method. The most straightforward is to make use of a `function`.

```Java
@Test
public void test10() {
  assertEquals(3, add(1, 2));
}

public int add(int a, int b) {
  return a + b;
}
```

A method is a code block. You can define a method anywhere, and it will be available in the context of the code. You can invoke a method from within another method.

```Java
@Test
public void test11() {
  assertEquals(6, add(2, 4));
}

public int add (int a, int b) {
  return mult (a, b);
}

public int mult(int a, int b) {
  return a * b;
}
```

A method can have parameters. A method definition is like a function definition. We define a parameter in the definition with a name and a type. Then in our function body all references to that parameter use that name when it is used. The first parameter we pass to `add` is `a`, and the second is `b`.

