To connect to the address, the certificate of the server must be verifiable. In this way, clients can verify the certificate of a server, and thus, the server can trust the client who uses the certificate. In reality, to obtain reliable server certificates, it may be necessary to perform man-in-the-middle tests, which is a complicated process.

Java provides the [HttpsURLConnection class](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/11/docs/api/java.net.http/java/net/http/HttpsURLConnection.html), which allows you to connect to a URL using an SSL socket. You can achieve the same effect by using the [URLConnection class](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/11/docs/api/java.base/java/net/URLConnection.html). The [SSLContext class](https://docs.oracle.com/en/java/javase/11/docs/api/java.base/javax/net/ssl/SSLContext.html) is also necessary.

