# Method Overloading

> A method is said to be overloaded if it has the same name as another method provided that the arguments of the new methods differ in their types by at least one parameter.

Java provides the capability of overloading methods. Simply put, overloading is the process of defining more than one methods with the same name in a class but with different parameters. This is sometimes referred to as compile-time polymorphism. This is useful in situations where a method must have different implementations for different types of input provided by the caller.

Consider the following code snippet:


```java
class Overloading {
    // Method overloading
    public static int calculate(int x, int y) {
        return x + y;
    }

    public static int calculate(int x) {
        return x * 2;
    }

    public static double calculate(double x, double y) {
        return x / y;
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Display result
        System.out.println("8+5 = " + calculate(8, 5));
        System.out.println("8*2 = " + calculate(8));
        System.out.println("9.5/4.0 = " + calculate(9.5, 4.0));

        // Invalid overloading
        // System.out.println("9.5/4.0 = " + calculate(9.5, 4.0));
    }
}
```

When the main() method is run, the following output is displayed on the console:

```text
8+5 = 13
8*2 = 16
9.5/4.0 = 2.4
```

Note that the main() method contains a statement that calls the calculate() method without supplying any parameters. This is allowed because the method is invoked using a method call expression

